Found by karr-worker while implementing #123, and it explains that ticket:
depends_on sat inert for so long because nothing on the CLI can write it.

Neither `karr create` nor `karr edit` has a --depends-on. The field is
reachable only through `karr import` of a kanban-md file view, or by editing
a materialized card by hand and importing it back. Every other field on the
task model has a CLI route.

So after #123 karr warns about a relationship that karr itself cannot express.
The tests for #123 had to seed depends_on through BoardStore directly for that
reason.

Shape: --depends-on on create and edit, comma-separated ids, matching how
--tags already works on both. Whether edit replaces or appends should follow
whatever --tags does, so there is one rule rather than two.

Once this exists, set-time validation becomes possible and worth having --
kanban-md does both (internal/task/validate.go:155, ValidateDependencyIDs
rejects a self-reference and an unknown id at the moment they are written).
The worker's judgement, which I agree with: set-time validation and the
move-time warning from #123 are complementary rather than alternatives.
Set-time catches a typo while the author still remembers what they meant;
move-time catches state that changed afterwards -- a dependency reopened or
deleted -- which set-time can never see. But set-time has nothing to attach to
until this ticket exists, so this one gates it.
